Colin</forename><surname>Raffel</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Barret</forename><surname>Zoph</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Sebastian</forename><surname>Borgeaud</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Dani</forename><surname>Yogatama</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Maarten</forename><surname>Bosma</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Denny</forename><surname>Zhou</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Donald</forename><surname>Metzler</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Ed</forename><forename type=\"middle\">H</forename><surname>Chi</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Tatsunori</forename><surname>Hashimoto</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Oriol</forename><surname>Vinyals</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Percy</forename><surname>Liang</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Jeff</forename><surname>Dean</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">William</forename><surname>Fedus</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t</analytic>\n\t<monogr>\n\t\t<title level=\"j\">Transactions on Machine Learning Research</title>\n\t\t<imprint>\n\t\t\t<date type=\"published\" when=\"2022\">2022</date>\n\t\t</imprint>\n\t</monogr>\n\t<note>Survey Certification</note>\n</biblStruct>\n\n<biblStruct status=\"extracted\" xml:id=\"b41\">\n\t<monogr>\n\t\t<title level=\"m\" type=\"main\">Chain-of-thought prompting elicits reasoning in large language models</title>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Jason</forename><surname>Wei</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Xuezhi</forename><surname>Wang</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Dale</forename><surname>Schuurmans</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Maarten</forename><surname>Bosma</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Brian</forename><surname>Ichter</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Fei</forename><surname>Xia</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Ed</forename><surname>Chi</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Quoc</forename><surname>Le</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Denny</forename><surname>Zhou</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<idno type=\"arXiv\">arXiv:2201.11903</idno>\n\t\t<imprint>\n\t\t\t<date type=\"published\" when=\"2023\">2023</date>\n\t\t</imprint>\n\t</monogr>\n\t<note type=\"report_type\">Preprint</note>\n</biblStruct>\n\n<biblStruct status=\"extracted\" xml:id=\"b42\">\n\t<analytic>\n\t\t<title level=\"a\" type=\"main\">Executing the code on the test input gives {&apos;R&apos;: &apos;Red&apos;, &apos;B&apos;: &apos;Blue&apos;, &apos;P&apos;: &apos;Pink&apos;, &apos;G&apos;: &apos;Green&apos;, &apos;W&apos;: &apos;White&apos;, &apos;L&apos;: &apos;lavender&apos;}, the same as the LLM&apos;s reasoning with Imagined Execution. Indeed, the LLM&apos;s reasoning is correct: the executed result and the expected result are not the same, where B should be mapped to Black instead of Blue. The synthesized code does not map the B key correctly, thus the synthesized code is wrong</title>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Shunyu</forename><surname>Yao</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Dian</forename><surname>Yu</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Jeffrey</forename><surname>Zhao</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Izhak</forename><surname>Shafran</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Tom</forename><surname>Griffiths</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Yuan</forename><surname>Cao</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Karthik</forename><surname>Narasimhan</surname></persName>\n\t\t</author>\n\t</analytic>\n\t<monogr>\n\t\t<title level=\"m\">Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems</title>\n\t\t<imprint>\n\t\t\t<date type=\"published\" when=\"2024\">2024</date>\n\t\t\t<biblScope unit=\"volume\">36</biblScope>\n\t\t</imprint>\n\t</monogr>\n\t<note>Tree of thoughts: Deliberate problem solving with large language models B&apos;: &apos;Black P&apos;: &apos;Pink&apos;}, {&apos;G&apos;: &apos; Green&apos;, &apos;W&apos;: &apos;White&apos;}, {&apos;L&apos;: &apos;lavender&apos;, &apos;B&apos;: &apos;Blue&apos;}) == ? Answer in &lt;test_case&gt; Given the reasoning above, complete the following test case. assert merge_dictionaries_three({&apos;R&apos;: &apos;Red P&apos;: &apos;Pink G&apos;: &apos;Green W&apos;: &apos;White &lt;/test_case&gt; tag. Output: assert merge_dictionaries_three({&apos;R&apos;: &apos;Red&apos;, &apos;B&apos;: &apos;Black&apos;, &apos;P&apos;: &apos;Pink&apos;}, {&apos;G&apos;: &apos; Green&apos;, &apos;W&apos;: &apos;White&apos;}, {&apos;L&apos;: &apos;lavender&apos;, &apos;B&apos;: &apos;Blue&apos;}) == {&apos;R&apos;: &apos;Red&apos;, &apos;B&apos;: &apos; Blue L&apos;: &apos;lavender&apos;} The canonical test case is &quot;assert merge_dictionaries_three({&apos;R&apos;: &apos;Red&apos;, &apos;B&apos;: &apos;Black&apos;, &apos;P&apos;: &apos;Pink&apos;}, {&apos;G&apos;: &apos;Green&apos;, &apos;W&apos;: &apos;White&apos;}, {&apos;L&apos;: &apos;lavender&apos;, &apos;B&apos;: &apos;Blue&apos;}) == {&apos;W&apos;: &apos;White&apos;, &apos;P&apos;: &apos;Pink&apos;, &apos;B&apos;: &apos;Black&apos;, &apos;R&apos;: &apos;Red&apos;, &apos;G&apos;: &apos;Green&apos;, &apos;L&apos;: &apos;lavender&apos;} and our Code Quality Checker correctly rejects this code. Note that the LLM has explained the mapping of B key explicitly, showing the strength of our Imagined Execution method in predicting synthesized code correctness</note>\n</biblStruct>\n\n\t\t\t\t</listBibl>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t</back>\n\t</text>\n</TEI>\n","pdf_hash":"84d290f09e843d48e46c1a24982c7ef69176038549479f7bdc015e8788670254"}